# Obsidian session state — changes every time the vault is opened, no
# meaningful content. Shared config (appearance/plugins) IS tracked on purpose.
.obsidian

# macOS Finder metadata
.DS_Store

# Personal/local Claude Code settings — not meant to be shared (like .env.local).
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent/skill tooling. Third-party skills are installed and updated per-machine,
# not vendored here: they are not ours to redistribute and are irrelevant to
# anyone reading this code. `skills-lock.json` is their install manifest and goes
# with them. Untracked 2026-09-11 after a scan found 158 files / 2.8 MB of
# third-party skill content — plus five symlinks into ~/.claude/skills/ that are
# dead for anyone who clones — published in this PUBLIC repo.
#
# REVERSED 2026-09-14: `.claude/agents/` was deliberately tracked on 2026-09-11
# ("they encode this project's review process, and they belong in history").
# That call is withdrawn — the whole of `.claude/` is now ignored, along with
# `CLAUDE.md`, `notes-logs/` and `.agents/`, matching what the project template
# and this project's own CLAUDE.md have always claimed the remote carries.
# The agent definitions live in the vault at Projects/(PROJECT TEMPLATE)/ and
# are copied per project, so git was never their home or their backup.
.claude/
skills-lock.json

# Third-party design skills under the older `.agents/` path. Same rationale as
# `.claude/skills/` above, and missed by the 2026-09-11 sweep: 12 files were
# still published in this PUBLIC repo.
.agents/

# Project-scoped Claude Code context. Prime directive, roadmap state, cost
# figures and working notes — written for this machine, not for a reader of the
# code. The template has always said the remote does not carry it; it did.
CLAUDE.md

# Local MCP server config. Project scope exists to share servers with
# collaborators; this is a solo build, so the file has no reader but this
# machine. It also points at a specific Supabase project and carries a
# read_only flag that gets toggled deliberately — state git should not record.
.mcp.json

# Next.js
/node_modules
/.next/
/out/
/build
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# env files
.env*
!.env.example

# Vercel
.vercel

# misc
*.pem
npm-debug.log*

# Structured cost records, and the generated HTML view of them.
#
# `notes-logs/cost/(C) COST.md` in the same directory IS committed, deliberately:
# it is the trend history, and `git diff` on it is how a cost regression gets
# noticed. These two are not. runs.jsonl is raw local measurement data and this
# is a PUBLIC repo — it carries no user id by construction (see toJsonlLine),
# but it still records one person's digest ids, run shapes and spend, and that
# belongs in git no more than the transcripts below did. report.html is a build
# artifact regenerated from it.
#
# Consequence accepted: the data behind a committed report is then local-only
# and unbacked. The Supabase usage_runs table is the durable copy.
#
# WIDENED 2026-09-14: the whole of `notes-logs/` is now ignored, not just the
# data files. project-log.md alone is ~53,000 words of working notes, and the
# two (C) cost-diagnosis records are dated internal analysis. `(C) COST.md` is
# the one deliberate exception — Tarek's call, kept tracked so `git diff` on it
# is still how a cost regression gets noticed. Re-including one file under an
# ignored directory needs the parent un-ignored first, hence the four lines.
notes-logs/*
!notes-logs/cost/
notes-logs/cost/*
!notes-logs/cost/(C) COST.md

# Raw dev-server transcripts from cost measurement runs. These were captured
# with `npm run dev | tee` — 667 KB of interleaved Next.js request logs with
# the [usage] lines buried in them. Superseded by the structured cost pipeline;
# kept out of git so measurement output never lands here as scrollback again.
# (Redundant since notes-logs/* above, kept as the record of why they were
# excluded in the first place.)
notes-logs/cost-test-log*
